  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with a mid-level ridge over the Rockies \nand northwest flow over the Great Plains. Clouds have cleared out \nover most of eastern Nebraska/western Iowa this evening. With calm \nto fairly light winds and cooling temperatures, fog development will \nbe possible, particularly for areas along the Missouri River and the \nIowa/Missouri border. A few models have been trying to bring some \nfog as far west as Lancaster and Gage counties for a few hours \nMonday morning. A few areas may have visibility reductions to 1 mile \nor less for a few hours around sunrise. \n\nA few isolated thunderstorms are expected to develop across southern \nSouth Dakota overnight, dropping into eastern Nebraska by sunrise \nMonday morning. A few areas may wake up to some showers or weak \nstorms moving through. High temperatures will reach the upper 70s to \nupper 80s across the region. A shortwave trough will move into the \nregion Monday afternoon, bringing some chances for showers and \nstorms. Moving into a decent environment, this first potential wave \nof storms will have the potential of becoming strong to severe. The \nmain hazards with any storms that are able to strengthen would be \nlarge hail, strong winds, and heavy rainfall which may result in \nlocalized flash flooding, particularly for areas that have already \nhad decent rainfall over the past few days. An isolated tornado risk \ncannot be entirely ruled out. A second wave of thunderstorm activity \nwill be possible after midnight Monday night/Tuesday morning. With \nthe earlier convection and a more stable environment, the \npossibility for severe weather will go down some with this later \nwave of storms. \n\nSurface low pressure develops over the Central High Plains on \nTuesday and a slow moving cold front arrives in northern Nebraska \nand Iowa. Expect some slightly cooler temperatures near the NE/SD \nborder, ranging from the mid-70s to low 80s for highs. Areas further \nsouth will top out in the low to mid-80s. Moisture will be moving \ninto the region and destabilization is expected over southeastern \nNebraska/southwestern Iowa during the afternoon. Strong to severe \nthunderstorms are expected Tuesday afternoon and evening across the \nentire Omaha CWA, with large hail, damaging winds, and a few \nisolated tornadoes all possible. Precipitable water values of 1.5-\n2.0\" are expected as well. Heavy rainfall with any storms may result \nin some localized flash flooding. Several areas across the region \nhave already received quite a bit of rainfall over the past few days \nand may therefore be more prone to flooding with any additional \nheavy rainfall.\n\nAn amplifying ridge over the western CONUS will send a series of \nweak shortwaves through the Great Plains Wednesday through Friday. \nThis will bring periodic chances (30-60% Wednesday; 20-30% Thursday \nand Friday) for showers and storms to the region. Wednesday high \ntemperatures will be fairly nice for August standards, reaching the \nmid-70s to low 80s. Typical highs for eastern Nebraska/western Iowa \nare in the mid-80s. Expect a gradual warming through Friday with \nhighs returning to the low to mid-80s.",
